*** Add File:docs/runbooks/vault_csi_and_cert_rotation.md
+# Vault CSI + SecretProviderClass install and cert rotation runbook
+
+This runbook walks you through installing the Vault CSI provider, creating a SecretProviderClass for Aegis, and performing a certificate rotation exercise (request new certs, mount via CSI, and verify server TLS).
+
+Prereqs
+- Kubernetes cluster with helm and kubectl access.
+- Vault server reachable and operator credentials.
+- Secrets Store CSI provider & Vault provider (secrets-store-csi-driver, provider-vault) access.
+
+Steps
+1. Install the Secrets Store CSI driver and Vault provider (example with helm):
+   - helm repo add secrets-store-csi-driver https://kubernetes-sigs.github.io/secrets-store-csi-driver/charts
+   - helm repo add hashicorp https://helm.releases.hashicorp.com
+   - helm install csi secrets-store-csi-driver/secrets-store-csi-driver -n kube-system
+   - install the Vault provider per provider docs (provider-vault)
+
+2. Bootstrap Vault PKI role (operator):
+   - export VAULT_ADDR=https://vault.example:8200
+   - export VAULT_TOKEN=<operator-token>
+   - Review and run ops/vault/pki_bootstrap.sh (edit role name and TTL settings)
+
+3. Create SecretProviderClass for Aegis:
+   - Template: ops/vault/secretproviderclass-dc.yaml (or ops/langgraph/k8s/oidc-configmap.yaml.template for OIDC config)
+   - Edit VAULT_ADDR, roleName and objects fields to match your PKI role and object names
+   - kubectl apply -f ops/vault/secretproviderclass-dc.yaml -n aegis
+
+4. Deploy workloads that mount certs:
+   - Update Helm values to use secretProviderClass (helm/aegis-dc/values.yaml or helm/aegis-grpc/values.yaml)
+   - helm upgrade --install aegis-dc helm/aegis-dc -n aegis -f values.yaml
+
+5. Perform cert rotation exercise:
+   - Run: python3 ops/vault/rotate_and_annotate.py (operator, requires vault/kubectl)
+   - This script requests a new cert from Vault PKI and patches deployments with an annotation to force rollout
+   - Confirm pods restart and check that certs appear under /etc/certs inside pods (or k8s Secret, depending on your flow)
+
+6. Verify TLS & mTLS:
+   - From inside cluster (exec into a pod), run:
+     - python3 -c "import ssl, socket; s=ssl.create_default_context(cafile='/etc/certs/ca.crt'); conn=s.wrap_socket(socket.socket(), server_hostname='aegis-async-inference'); conn.connect(('127.0.0.1',50054)); print('TLS OK')"
+   - For client mTLS, ensure client uses mounted client cert/key and verifies server ca.
+
+7. Post-rotation checks:
+   - Check logs for errors related to cert parsing or TLS handshake failures.
+   - Validate services that rely on TLS are healthy (readiness/liveness probes).
+   - Verify evidence manifests for rotation event (ops/security/key_rotation_playbook.md suggests recording manifest).
+
+Notes & troubleshooting
+- If pods fail to mount CSI volumes, check that the Secrets Store CSI driver is installed and has permissions.
+- If Vault denies pki/issue/<role>, check role permissions and TTL.
+- For production, prefer Secrets Store CSI + automount into pods rather than writing k8s Secrets with private keys.
+
